  • if notes[i]>notes[i+1] then b_i>b_{i+1},
  • if notes[i]=notes[i+1] then b_i≠b_{i+1}.

A simple strategy is to use b_i=notes[i] mod 5 + 1.  
This works because:
  • if notes[i]<notes[i+1] then (notes[i] mod 5)<(notes[i+1] mod 5), so b_i<b_{i+1},
  • if notes[i]>notes[i+1] then (notes[i] mod 5)>(notes[i+1] mod 5), so b_i>b_{i+1},
  • if notes[i]=notes[i+1] then (notes[i] mod 5)=(notes[i+1] mod 5), so b_i=b_{i+1}, but since notes[i]=notes[i+1] the third condition is never violated.

Thus this fingering is always convenient.

